<p><a href="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-02-17-long-story-1.png"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-02-17-long-story-1.png" alt="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%BCller-Lyer_Illusion_-_MathWorld_version.svg" width="960" height="576" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-72713" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-02-17-long-story-1.png 960w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-02-17-long-story-1-600x360.png 600w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/2026-02-17-long-story-1-300x180.png 300w" sizes="(max-width: 960px) 100vw, 960px" /></a></p>
<p>This is a familiar variation on the Müller-Lyer optical illusion. The horizontal segments on the left appear longer than those on the right, but in fact they&#8217;re all the same length.</p>
<p>Italian researcher Gianni A. Sarcone devised the dynamic demonstration below &#8212; the blue and black segments are all the same length and do not change; only the &#8220;fins&#8221; are moving:</p>
